---
Part XIII, Chapter 2: The kinds tropes declare
Package: packages/loom-lang/
Language: TypeScript
---

# The kinds tropes declare

A trope extends Loom's language by declaring kinds. Under contract 1 a kind is a kind of box or a kind of section. A trope that declares the `Mermaid` kind of box lets every loom of the book write a box `::: Cells {Mermaid}`. Before any trope declares it, such a box is a fault, so a misspelt kind never passes for content. A trope that declares the `Option` kind of section lets every loom write a heading `## Postgres {Option}`, with the parameters the kind takes.

Loom applies what tropes declare without running any trope. It gathers every trope's declared kinds into the registry, a set of tables it consults for each box and each heading of the book, and it refuses a trope's root whose manifest it cannot read or whose kinds clash with kinds already taken.

A declared kind of section changes how a loom is read, so the registry must exist before any loom is built with it. Loom therefore gathers the registry from the chapters of tropes alone, each trope's root and its members, each read with core Loom. It then builds every loom with what the registry holds, as the chapter "The compile layer" describes. A manifest is read the same whatever its own trope declares. It draws only on sections of those chapters, so a trope may spread what it declares over its members, and a chapter of no trope lends a manifest nothing. Every step is Loom's own code, a lookup and a composition in memory, so the registry fits wherever Loom judges a loom: in the editor on every keystroke, and before every tangle.

## The registry

`DeclaredKinds` is the registry. It holds the `Declarations` of every trope, and `refusals`, every root Loom refuses and why. A `Trope` gathers one trope's chapters: its name, its `chapters`, every chapter that names the trope, and its `root`, when it has one.

`Declarations` holds a table for each sort of kind: `box` and `section`. A `DeclaredBox` records a kind of box: its name, the format of its body, and the trope that declared it, with the loom of the trope's root. A `DeclaredSection` records a kind of section: its name, the `parameters` a heading of the kind may carry, and the same trope and loom. `noDeclarations` holds two empty tables. A `Refusal` records a root's loom, the reason Loom refuses it, and the place in the loom the reason concerns.

=>

export type Trope = {
  readonly name: string
  readonly chapters: ReadonlyArray<LoomModule>
  readonly root: Option.Option<LoomModule>
}

export type DeclaredBox = {
  readonly kind: string
  readonly body: string
  readonly trope: string
  readonly module: Location
}

export type DeclaredSection = {
  readonly kind: string
  readonly parameters: Keys
  readonly trope: string
  readonly module: Location
}

export type Declarations = {
  readonly box: ReadonlyArray<DeclaredBox>
  readonly section: ReadonlyArray<DeclaredSection>
}

export const noDeclarations: Declarations = { box: [], section: [] }

export type Refusal = { readonly module: Location; readonly reason: string; readonly at: Position }

export type DeclaredKinds = Declarations & { readonly refusals: ReadonlyArray<Refusal> }

## A trope's root and its manifest

A trope is a cluster of chapters, and each of them names the trope in its frontmatter. The chapter that roots the trope is a trope chapter, and its title is the trope's name; `rootsTrope` tells such a chapter. Every other chapter of the trope is a member, and names the trope with a `Rooted in:` line. `tropeOf` reads the trope a chapter roots or belongs to, and `tropeLineOf` finds where it says so: the word `Trope` of a trope chapter, or the value of a `Rooted in:` line. `tropesOf` gathers each trope's chapters in the order of their paths, so that when two chapters clash, the same one stands on every machine. A trope whose chapters are all members has no root yet.

The root is also where the trope's manifest belongs, a section of the `Manifest` kind, which `manifestSectionsOf` finds. A manifest anywhere else is the product pass's to fault, as the chapter "The product pass" describes. A root with two manifests leaves Loom unsure which to read, so Loom refuses it. `manifestOf` composes the one manifest into its text with the product pass's own composition, `fromCode`, so an anchor in the manifest draws in the code it names. The composed text then goes to `readManifest`, as the chapter "The trope contract" describes. Each refusal of a manifest stands at the manifest's heading.

A manifest is composed against the chapters of tropes alone, so an anchor that names a section of any other chapter finds nothing there. A composition leaves such an anchor's place empty, and the manifest would read as if the author had declared less. `composedOf` therefore asks `unfoundIn` for the names the composition cannot find, and refuses the manifest for the first of them.

=>

const tropeLine = (module: LoomModule): Option.Option<FrontmatterTropeToken> =>
  Option.orElse(Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter?.trope), () =>
    Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter?.rootedIn),
  )

export const tropeOf = (module: LoomModule): Option.Option<string> =>
  Option.map(tropeLine(module), (line) => line.value)

export const tropeLineOf = (module: LoomModule): Position =>
  Option.getOrElse(
    Option.map(tropeLine(module), (line) => line.position),
    () => module.doc.position,
  )

export const rootsTrope = (module: LoomModule): boolean =>
  module.doc.frontmatter?.trope !== undefined

export const tropesOf = (modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>): ReadonlyArray<Trope> => {
  const named = pipe(
    Array.fromIterable(modules.values()),
    Array.filter((module) => Option.isSome(tropeOf(module))),
    Array.sort(Order.mapInput(Order.String, (module: LoomModule) => module.path)),
  )
  return Array.map(Array.dedupe(Array.getSomes(Array.map(named, tropeOf))), (name): Trope => {
    const chapters = Array.filter(named, (module) => Option.contains(tropeOf(module), name))
    return { name, chapters, root: Array.findFirst(chapters, rootsTrope) }
  })
}

const manifestSectionsOf = (module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<LoomSection> =>
  Array.filter(module.doc.sections, isManifest)

type Read = { readonly manifest: TropeManifest; readonly at: Position }

const composedOf = (
  modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>,
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  code: Code,
): Result.Result<string, string> =>
  Array.match(unfoundIn(modules, code, catalogue), {
    onEmpty: () => Result.succeed(fromCode(modules, code, catalogue).code),
    onNonEmpty: ([name]) =>
      Result.fail(`its manifest draws on \`${name}\`, which no chapter of a trope holds`),
  })

const manifestOf = (
  modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>,
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  root: LoomModule,
  manifests: Array.NonEmptyReadonlyArray<LoomSection>,
): Result.Result<Read, Refusal> => {
  const [section, ...others] = manifests
  const refusal = (reason: string, at: Position): Refusal => ({ module: root.path, reason, at })
  return Array.match(others, {
    onNonEmpty: ([second]) =>
      Result.fail(refusal('it holds more than one manifest', second.heading.position)),
    onEmpty: () =>
      pipe(
        Array.findFirst(root.product.code, (code) => code.origin.name === sectionNameOf(section)),
        Option.match({
          onNone: (): Result.Result<string, string> => Result.succeed(''),
          onSome: (code) => composedOf(modules, catalogue, code),
        }),
        Result.flatMap(readManifest),
        Result.map((manifest): Read => ({ manifest, at: section.heading.position })),
        Result.mapError((reason) => refusal(reason, section.heading.position)),
      ),
  })
}

## Refusing a clash

A kind means one thing in a book, so two tropes cannot both decide what one kind means. `clashOf` takes the kinds a manifest declares for one table, and looks for the first that is already taken. The core keeps its own kinds for itself, so a trope that declares the `Note` kind of box is refused, and so is one that declares the `Tangle` kind of section. A kind an earlier trope declared is taken too, and the root that comes later in the order of paths is refused, with the earlier trope named. A root that declares one kind twice is refused as well. Kinds compare in any case of letters, as braces read them.

Each table is judged alone. A box and a section stand in different places, so a kind of box and a kind of section may share a name.

A key means one thing on a heading as well. `ownKeyOf` looks for a parameter that a kind of section declares under one of the core's own keys, `lang` or `kind`, and such a root is refused. `refusalIn` gives the first reason a manifest is refused: a clash among its kinds of box, a clash among its kinds of section, or a key of the core's.

=>

const sameKind = (one: string, other: string): boolean => one.toLowerCase() === other.toLowerCase()

type Taken = { readonly kind: string; readonly trope: string }

const clashOf = (
  declared: ReadonlyArray<string>,
  core: ReadonlyArray<string>,
  taken: ReadonlyArray<Taken>,
): Option.Option<string> =>
  Array.findFirst(declared, (kind, at) =>
    Match.value({
      core: Array.some(core, (own) => sameKind(own, kind)),
      earlier: Array.findFirst(taken, (other) => sameKind(other.kind, kind)),
      twice: Array.some(Array.take(declared, at), (before) => sameKind(before, kind)),
    }).pipe(
      Match.when({ core: true }, () => Option.some(`\`${kind}\` is one of Loom's own kinds`)),
      Match.when({ earlier: Option.isSome }, ({ earlier }) =>
        Option.map(earlier, (other) => `the \`${other.trope}\` trope declares \`${kind}\` already`),
      ),
      Match.when({ twice: true }, () => Option.some(`it declares \`${kind}\` twice`)),
      Match.orElse(() => Option.none<string>()),
    ),
  )

const parametersOf = (declaration: SectionKindDeclaration): Keys =>
  Option.getOrElse(Option.fromNullishOr(declaration.parameters), (): Keys => ({}))

const ownKeyOf = (declared: ReadonlyArray<SectionKindDeclaration>): Option.Option<string> =>
  pipe(
    Array.flatMap(declared, (declaration) => Object.keys(parametersOf(declaration))),
    Array.findFirst((key) => Option.isSome(keyType(headingKeys, key))),
    Option.map((key) => `\`${key}\` is one of Loom's own keys`),
  )

const kindsIn = (declared: ReadonlyArray<{ readonly kind: string }>): ReadonlyArray<string> =>
  Array.map(declared, (declaration) => declaration.kind)

const refusalIn = (manifest: TropeManifest, taken: Declarations): Option.Option<string> =>
  pipe(
    clashOf(kindsIn(boxKindsOf(manifest)), boxKinds, taken.box),
    Option.orElse(() => clashOf(kindsIn(sectionKindsOf(manifest)), sectionKinds, taken.section)),
    Option.orElse(() => ownKeyOf(sectionKindsOf(manifest))),
  )

## Gathering the registry

`declaredKindsOf` builds the registry from a corpus. It gathers the tropes and folds over them, carrying the registry so far, and `withTrope` adds one trope. A workspace may also install tropes from its store, as the chapter "Tropes a workspace installs" describes, and their declarations arrive as `installed`. The fold starts from them, so a trope of the book that declares a kind an installed trope declares is refused, as any later clash is. One trope keeps one root, so a second trope chapter of the same title is refused, at the word `Trope` of its identity line. `titleOf` reads a chapter's title.

`withRoot` reads the root's manifest, when the root holds one. A root whose manifest reads and clashes with nothing adds every kind it declares, and `boxesOf` and `sectionsOf` turn them into rows of the registry. A root Loom refuses adds its refusal and none of its kinds, since the author agreed to the trope as a whole, and a trope that runs half its offer is not the trope the author agreed to.

=>

export const titleOf = (module: LoomModule): string =>
  Option.getOrElse(
    Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter?.title), (title) => title.value),
    () => module.path,
  )

const boxesOf = (
  trope: string,
  root: LoomModule,
  manifest: TropeManifest,
): ReadonlyArray<DeclaredBox> =>
  Array.map(boxKindsOf(manifest), (declaration): DeclaredBox => ({
    kind: declaration.kind,
    body: declaration.body,
    trope,
    module: root.path,
  }))

const sectionsOf = (
  trope: string,
  root: LoomModule,
  manifest: TropeManifest,
): ReadonlyArray<DeclaredSection> =>
  Array.map(sectionKindsOf(manifest), (declaration): DeclaredSection => ({
    kind: declaration.kind,
    parameters: parametersOf(declaration),
    trope,
    module: root.path,
  }))

const refused = (kinds: DeclaredKinds, refusal: Refusal): DeclaredKinds => ({
  ...kinds,
  refusals: [...kinds.refusals, refusal],
})

const withRoot = (
  modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>,
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  kinds: DeclaredKinds,
  trope: string,
  root: LoomModule,
): DeclaredKinds =>
  Array.match(manifestSectionsOf(root), {
    onEmpty: () => kinds,
    onNonEmpty: (manifests) =>
      Result.match(manifestOf(modules, catalogue, root, manifests), {
        onFailure: (refusal) => refused(kinds, refusal),
        onSuccess: ({ manifest, at }) =>
          Option.match(refusalIn(manifest, kinds), {
            onSome: (reason) => refused(kinds, { module: root.path, reason, at }),
            onNone: () => ({
              ...kinds,
              box: [...kinds.box, ...boxesOf(trope, root, manifest)],
              section: [...kinds.section, ...sectionsOf(trope, root, manifest)],
            }),
          }),
      }),
  })

const withTrope = (
  modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>,
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  kinds: DeclaredKinds,
  trope: Trope,
): DeclaredKinds => {
  const others = Array.filter(
    trope.chapters,
    (chapter) => !Option.exists(trope.root, (root) => root.path === chapter.path),
  )
  const duplicates = Option.match(trope.root, {
    onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<Refusal> => [],
    onSome: (root) =>
      Array.map(Array.filter(others, rootsTrope), (chapter): Refusal => ({
        module: chapter.path,
        reason: `another trope chapter is called \`${titleOf(root)}\``,
        at: tropeLineOf(chapter),
      })),
  })
  const settled: DeclaredKinds = { ...kinds, refusals: [...kinds.refusals, ...duplicates] }
  return Option.match(trope.root, {
    onNone: () => settled,
    onSome: (root) => withRoot(modules, catalogue, settled, trope.name, root),
  })
}

export const declaredKindsOf = (
  modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>,
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  installed: Declarations = noDeclarations,
): DeclaredKinds => {
  const fromInstalled: DeclaredKinds = { ...installed, refusals: [] }
  return Array.reduce(tropesOf(modules), fromInstalled, (kinds, trope) =>
    withTrope(modules, catalogue, kinds, trope),
  )
}

## Judging a loom against the registry

`kindFaults` gives a loom the faults the registry finds in it. A root that Loom refused carries a `TropeRefused` fault with the reason, where the refusal stands.

Every box of the loom that names a kind is judged against the core's kinds and the declared ones. `writtenKind` reads the kind a box's braces name, and where: a label's words, or the value of a `kind` parameter. A label that the tokeniser has already faulted, such as an empty one, is judged no further. A kind that neither the core nor a trope gives a box carries an `UnknownKind` fault, which lists every kind the box could take, the core's first and the declared ones after them.

A box's keys depend on its kind as well. Only a trope box takes `provides` and `anchors`, so `keysOutsideTrope` gives either key on a box of any other kind a `KeyOutsideTrope` fault, on the key itself.

=>

const writtenKind = (
  specifier: SpecifierToken | ParametersToken,
): Option.Option<{ readonly kind: string; readonly at: Position }> =>
  Match.value(specifier).pipe(
    Match.when({ type: 'Specifier' }, (labelled) =>
      labelled.label.health.status === 'ok'
        ? Option.some({ kind: labelled.label.value, at: labelled.label.position })
        : Option.none(),
    ),
    Match.when({ type: 'Parameters' }, (held) =>
      pipe(
        Array.findFirst(held.parameters, (parameter) => parameter.key.value === 'kind'),
        Option.flatMap((parameter) => Array.head(parameter.values)),
        Option.map((value) => ({ kind: value.value, at: value.position })),
      ),
    ),
    Match.exhaustive,
  )

export const kindFaults = (kinds: DeclaredKinds, module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> => {
  const known = [...boxKinds, ...Array.map(kinds.box, (declared) => declared.kind)]
  const refused = pipe(
    kinds.refusals,
    Array.filter((refusal) => refusal.module === module.path),
    Array.flatMap(
      (refusal) => faulty(TropeRefused({ reason: refusal.reason }), refusal.at).diagnostics,
    ),
  )
  const unknown = pipe(
    openedBoxesOf(module.doc),
    Array.flatMap((opened) => Option.toArray(Option.fromNullishOr(opened.box.specifier))),
    Array.flatMap((specifier) => Option.toArray(writtenKind(specifier))),
    Array.filter(({ kind }) => !Array.some(known, (each) => sameKind(each, kind))),
    Array.flatMap(
      ({ kind, at }) => faulty(UnknownKind({ kind, kinds: known, on: 'box' }), at).diagnostics,
    ),
  )
  return [...refused, ...unknown, ...keysOutsideTrope(module)]
}

const keysOutsideTrope = (module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> =>
  pipe(
    openedBoxesOf(module.doc),
    Array.filter((opened) => boxKind(opened.box) !== tropeKind),
    Array.flatMap((opened) =>
      Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(opened.box.specifier), {
        onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<ParameterToken> => [],
        onSome: (specifier) => (specifier.type === 'Parameters' ? specifier.parameters : []),
      }),
    ),
    Array.filter((parameter) => ['provides', 'anchors'].includes(parameter.key.value)),
    Array.flatMap(
      (parameter) =>
        faulty(KeyOutsideTrope({ key: parameter.key.value }), parameter.key.position).diagnostics,
    ),
  )

## What the registry draws on

From `effect` come the array, option, and result combinators, `Match` for the clashes and the kinds a box's braces name, `Order` for the order of the tropes, and `pipe` for the steps. From the Loom AST come the `Diagnostic` and `Position` a fault lands with, the section a manifest is, the `boxKinds` and `sectionKinds` the core keeps, the `headingKeys` it keeps with `keyType` and the `Keys` a kind's parameters are, `boxKind` and `tropeKind`, which tell a trope box, the `FrontmatterTropeToken` a chapter names its trope with, and the tokens a box's braces arrive in, and from the corpus the `Catalogue`, the modules, and `openedBoxesOf`, which lists a loom's boxes. The product pass lends `isManifest` and `sectionNameOf`, which find a manifest's composed code, and the projection lends `fromCode`, which composes it, and `unfoundIn`, which names what the composition cannot find, over the `Code` the product model gives. The fault catalog lends `faulty` with the `TropeRefused`, `UnknownKind`, and `KeyOutsideTrope` faults, and the contract lends `readManifest` with the shapes it reads and the two functions that read a manifest's tables.

=>

import { Array, Match, Option, Order, pipe, Result } from 'effect'
import type { Diagnostic, Position } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomNode'
import type { LoomSection } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomAst'
import {
  boxKind,
  boxKinds,
  type FrontmatterTropeToken,
  headingKeys,
  type Keys,
  keyType,
  type ParameterToken,
  type ParametersToken,
  sectionKinds,
  type SpecifierToken,
  tropeKind,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomTokens'
import {
  type Catalogue,
  type Location,
  type LoomModule,
  openedBoxesOf,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomCorpusAst'
import { isManifest, sectionNameOf } from './ProductBuilder'
import type { Code } from '@athrio/loom-ast/ProductAst'
import { fromCode, unfoundIn } from './LoomVirtualCodeBuilder'
import { faulty, KeyOutsideTrope, TropeRefused, UnknownKind } from './LoomFault'
import {
  boxKindsOf,
  readManifest,
  type SectionKindDeclaration,
  sectionKindsOf,
  type TropeManifest,
} from './TropeManifest'

## The module [src/ast/DeclaredKinds.ts]

=>

